# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5.memetic-wikitext" "A sigil names its own verb, and a bearing arrow survives inside one"

# THE DIALECT NAMES NOTHING FOR THE THING IT EXISTS TO SAY. TiddlyWiki reads `<<~ set …>>` as a call
# on a variable spelled `~`, so the verb binds as a POSITIONAL argument and paints exactly like any
# unquoted value a carrier writes. `set`, `oracle`, `lares` and `stage` all read `string.unquoted`.
#
# The base grammar must keep its reading — the host builds a transclusion here and nothing else —
# so the dialect names the verb BESIDE what the base says rather than instead of it.

<<~ set hud="aim" mode="scan">>
#   ^^^ entity.name.function.sigil.memetic-wikitext
#       ^^^ entity.other.attribute-name.argument.tiddlywiki5

<<~ oracle "a reading">>
#   ^^^^^^ entity.name.function.sigil.memetic-wikitext

# A CLOSER NAMES THE SIGIL IT CLOSES. TiddlyWiki reads `~/ahu` as one variable name, so the base
# offers a reader nothing to tell a closing sigil from an opening one.

<<~/ahu>>
#   ^^^ entity.name.function.sigil.memetic-wikitext

# A BEARING ARROW SPLITS IN TWO INSIDE A SIGIL. The base grammar's unquoted-value rule takes the
# dash before any lower-priority injection looks, and the closing angle falls to the parameters
# region — so `->` arrives as two tokens and neither carries the arrow's name. Every aim and yield
# line this house writes carries this form, and the only pinned specimen writes the PROSE one.

<<~ lares aim from="lar://a@b/c.d.e" -> to="?">>
#                                    ^^ keyword.operator.bearing.arrow.memetic-wikitext

# The control: the same arrow in prose, which already reads.

A bearing arrow -> stands named in prose.
#               ^^ keyword.operator.bearing.arrow.memetic-wikitext
